BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 576.0 Tm (Theorem. For every integer m >= 1, infinitely many positive integers K satisfy: for each j =) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 563.5 Tm (0,1,...,m-1, the integer 24\(K+j\)+1 is composite and is not a perfect square. In particular,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 551.0 Tm (K,K+1,...,K+m-1 are consecutive members of A202137.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11.4 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 524.5 Tm (2. Proof) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 508.5 Tm (Fix m >= 1. Choose distinct primes q_0,...,q_\(m-1\), each greater than 3. Because 24 is invertible) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 496.0 Tm (modulo q_j^2, the linear congruence 24\(K+j\)+1 = q_j \(mod q_j^2\) has a unique solution for K modulo) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 483.5 Tm (q_j^2, for each j.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 464.0 Tm (The moduli q_j^2 are pairwise coprime. The Chinese remainder theorem supplies an integer R that) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 451.5 Tm (satisfies all these congruences simultaneously. Every integer K = R+tM, with M the product of the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 439.0 Tm (q_j^2 and t any integer, is also a solution.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 419.5 Tm (For each j, let N_j = 24\(K+j\)+1. Since N_j = q_j \(mod q_j^2\), the prime q_j divides N_j, but q_j^2) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 407.0 Tm (does not. Thus q_j occurs in the prime factorization of N_j to exponent exactly one; N_j is not a) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 394.5 Tm (square.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 375.0 Tm (For all sufficiently large positive t we also have K >= 1 and N_j > q_j for every j. Hence q_j is a) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 362.5 Tm (proper prime divisor of N_j, so N_j is composite. Therefore K through K+m-1 belong to A202137. As t) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 350.0 Tm (grows these yield infinitely many distinct runs. QED.) Tj ET
